  annotation = {中文辅助译名：在线接受和承诺疗法自助课程通过抑制控制减少过度游戏行为的有效性：一项随机对照试验},
  abstract = {Background and aims: Internet Gaming Disorder (IGD) has emerged as a significant mental health concern. However, a substantial number of young people exhibit excessive gaming behavior (EGB) without meeting the full criteria for IGD. Compared to IGD, studies on EGB are limited. On the basis of innovative design acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) online self-help course, this study explored the mechanism of ACT self-help course improving inhibitory control ability and alleviating EGB. Methods: This study focused on college students with Internet Gaming Disorder-20 Test (IGD-20) scores between 55 and 70 and more than 21 h of weekly gaming time, representing individuals in the EGB stage who had not yet reached IGD. A total of 58 individuals with EGB (mean age = 20.5 ± 1.2 years) underwent an ACT self-help course (n = 30) or a routine education intervention (n = 28). The IGD-20, Behavior Rating Inventory of Executive Function-Adult Version (BRIEF-A), and Acceptance and Action Questionnaire (AAQ-II) were used to assess participants before and after the intervention. GO/NOGO tasks and electroencephalogram activities (N200, P300) were also recorded. Results: Compared to the control group, the ACT group exhibited significantly lower game addictive behaviors (IGD-20 score), reduced game time, and improved executive function (BRIEF-A) and cognitive flexibility (AAQ-II). Additionally, the ACT group demonstrated significantly improved NOGO task accuracy, increased N200 amplitude, and},
}

@misc{panorama_xiaochu_zhang_a6b77c0491362bf0,
  title = {Safety and efficacy of high-current tTIS targeting left sgACC for major depressive disorder: A randomized, double-blind, sham-controlled trial},
  author = {Mengmeng Wang and Wei Wu and Quanming Zheng and Hao Zhou and Jiecheng Ren and Huixing Gou and Xueer Meng and Guanbao Cui and Jingjing Sun and Jiahui Zhou and Xiyun Chen and Jian Guo and Shikang Li and Zhen He and Dexue Ge and Gang Wang and Qiuyu Yuan and Dongliang Jiao and Xinming Yu and Bin Wang and Mingsong Zhang and Chuangchuang Chang and Ginger Qinghong Zeng and Xiaochu Zhang},
  year = {2026},
  doi = {10.1016/j.xcrm.2026.103112},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2026.103112},
  howpublished = {Cell Reports Medicine},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：针对左 sgACC 的高电流 tTIS 治疗重度抑郁症的安全性和有效性：一项随机、双盲、假对照试验},
  abstract = {Major depressive disorder (MDD) is a leading cause of disability, and non-invasive stimulation of deep brain circuits such as the subgenual anterior cingulate cortex (sgACC) remains challenging. This randomized, double-blind, sham-controlled trial recruits 96 adults with moderate-to-severe MDD who received 10 sessions of 2 mA low-current, 15 mA high-current, or sham transcranial temporal interference stimulation (tTIS) targeting the left sgACC. The high-current group shows significantly greater reductions in depressive symptoms (17-item Hamilton Depression Rating Scale [HDRS-17] score) post-treatment and at 1-month follow-up compared with sham and outperforms the low-current group post-treatment. Exploratory Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) analyses suggest improved sleep quality in both active groups, whereas anxiety outcomes show no intergroup differences. High-current tTIS is safe and well tolerated, with only transient tingling. These findings provide preliminary evidence that sgACC-targeted tTIS relieves depressive symptoms safely, with more robust short-to medium-term effects observed under the 15 mA protocol. This trial registers on Chictr.org.cn (ChiCTR2400094971).},
}

