  abstract = {Brain cue-reactivity detectable with MRI in severe alcohol use disorder (AUD) is well characterized. However, the differences of inpatients’ brain reactivity to alcohol cues in the treatment process still need robust prediction. Therefore, we propose a hypothesis-free data-driven analysis method to identify the subtypes of AUD inpatients undergoing alcohol treatment protocol by the patient's brain responses towards the alcohol images to classify the inpatients according to their brain activity related to seeing alcohol cues. Thirty inpatients undergoing treatment in the hospital were recruited. Using fMRI, their brain activity was scanned while seeing alcohol and matched non-alcohol images. The beta values of brain responses to alcohol were extracted and forwarded to hierarchical clustering analysis. Using the proposed method, we identified two subtypes of patients. Patients grouped in pattern 1 activations showed significant brain activations in multiple brain areas, mainly in the mesolimbic and frontal regions. Patients in pattern 2 showed no significant increased activations and only showed decreased activations, mainly in the occipital regions. Even in the treatment process, there is evidence of brain activity in alcohol addiction-associated brain regions, which can help in predicting relapse and allocate inpatients to more optimized treatment. Cue-reactivity data-driven clustering analysis can detect subgroups of alcohol inpatients. It can help identify different AUD phe},
